From: linux-omarchy-aarch64
Date: Sun, 22 Nov 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] arm64: dts: qcom: x1p42100-asus-vivobook-s15: load the X Plus
 Iris firmware

7.3's x1p42100-asus-vivobook-s15.dts asks Iris for qcvss8380.mbn. ASUS
package 44984 (S5507QAD V1.367.7800.0) ships that name as the X Elite
image: MBN v7, QC_IMAGE_VERSION_STRING vfw-3.1 rel0088, variant PROD,
and a single soc_hw id 0xA009. Secure PAS rejects it on the X1P-42-100
(SoC hardware id 0xA016) with -EINVAL, so qcom-iris never comes up.

The same package's qcvss8380_pa.mbn is the one-pipe image the other X
Plus boards load (Dell Inspiron 14 5441, Samsung Galaxy Book4 Edge 15,
IdeaPad 5 2-in-1, IdeaPad Slim 3x). It is the same vfw-3.1 rel0088
build, variant PROD_IRIS3_1P, signed only for 0xA016. On an S5507QA,
installing those bytes and decoding H.264 with mpv --hwdec=v4l2m2m-copy
ran through iris_driver with no kernel errors (linux-omarchy 7.3.0-47,
21 November 2026).

Point this tree at qcvss8380_pa.mbn. The X Elite Vivobook S 15 keeps
qcvss8380.mbn.

---
diff --git a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/x1p42100-asus-vivobook-s15.dts b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/x1p42100-asus-vivobook-s15.dts
--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/x1p42100-asus-vivobook-s15.dts
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/x1p42100-asus-vivobook-s15.dts
@@ -24,7 +24,7 @@
 };
 
 &iris {
-	firmware-name = "qcom/x1p42100/ASUSTeK/vivobook-s15/qcvss8380.mbn";
+	firmware-name = "qcom/x1p42100/ASUSTeK/vivobook-s15/qcvss8380_pa.mbn";
 
 	status = "okay";
 };
